IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `ap-dates`
IMPORT `ap-duties`
IMPORT `ap-survival`
IMPORT `ap-intestacy`
IMPORT `ap-family-provision`

-- Administration and Probate Act 1958 (Vic) -- the questions of the CLAIMANT.
--
-- SEAT: the individual who claims on or through a deceased person's estate, or whose claim meets a death: the family member
-- or other person seeking family provision (Part IV); the partner or relative taking on an intestacy (Part IA); the injured
-- person whose wrongdoer has died, and the family or executor of an injured person who died (s 29); the person served with
-- the executor's notice to take proceedings (s 30). "Can I claim?", "Am I an eligible person?", "By when must I apply?",
-- "What do I take?", "Does the claim survive?", "Can the estate recover for pain and suffering?". The personal representative,
-- the Court and the Crown get no seat: their acts and judgments are asked as facts.
--
-- Every question takes plain facts (yes/no, numbers, dates, a choice from a list) and calls the rules of ap-survival.l4,
-- ap-intestacy.l4 and ap-family-provision.l4. The four steps with a deadline (ss 30, 70Q, 70R, 99) are DEONTIC rules run
-- inside (ap-duties.l4); no question takes a DEONTIC or an EVENT.
--
-- UNOFFICIAL VERSION: quotations from a plain-text conversion by Legalese Commons of the Word file of the Administration
-- and Probate Act 1958, Version No. 130 (22 October 2025), not the authorised version. (c) State of Victoria; published with
-- the authority of the Government Printer for the State of Victoria. No part may be reproduced except in accordance with
-- the Copyright Act 1968 (Cth). Quoted under the owner's ruling of 24 October 2026 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md).
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `The claimant's questions`

-- Builders: the seat's plain answers into the rule modules' records (not exported).
GIVEN `partners` IS A NUMBER
      `child lines` IS A NUMBER
      `issue not of a partner` IS A BOOLEAN
      `parents` IS A NUMBER
      `sibling lines` IS A NUMBER
      `remote sibling issue` IS A BOOLEAN
      `grandparents` IS A NUMBER
      `aunt and uncle lines` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A `The intestate's family`
`the family of` `partners` `child lines` `issue not of a partner` `parents` `sibling lines` `remote sibling issue` `grandparents` `aunt and uncle lines` MEANS
    `The intestate's family` WITH
        `the number of partners` IS `partners`
        `the number of child lines` IS `child lines`
        `some issue is not issue of a partner` IS `issue not of a partner`
        `the number of parents` IS `parents`
        `the number of sibling lines` IS `sibling lines`
        `a sibling's grandchild or remoter issue survives` IS `remote sibling issue`
        `the number of grandparents` IS `grandparents`
        `the number of aunt and uncle lines` IS `aunt and uncle lines`

GIVEN `relationship` IS A `My relationship to the deceased`
      `day of death` IS A DATE
      `registered` IS A BOOLEAN
      `living together` IS A BOOLEAN
      `years together` IS A NUMBER
      `parent of a child under 18` IS A BOOLEAN
      `age` IS A NUMBER
      `student` IS A BOOLEAN
      `impairment` IS A BOOLEAN
      `permanent` IS A BOOLEAN
      `reduced capacity` IS A BOOLEAN
      `participation affected` IS A BOOLEAN
      `could have taken FLA proceedings` IS A BOOLEAN
      `not taken or finalised` IS A BOOLEAN
      `prevented by death` IS A BOOLEAN
      `child has died` IS A BOOLEAN
      `day child died` IS A DATE
      `household member` IS A BOOLEAN
      `past household member likely to return` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Facts about me and the deceased`
`the facts about me` `relationship` `day of death` `registered` `living together` `years together` `parent of a child under 18` `age` `student` `impairment` `permanent` `reduced capacity` `participation affected` `could have taken FLA proceedings` `not taken or finalised` `prevented by death` `child has died` `day child died` `household member` `past household member likely to return` MEANS
    `Facts about me and the deceased` WITH
        `the relationship` IS `relationship`
        `the day of the death` IS `day of death`
        `registered domestic relationship` IS `registered`
        `living together as a couple at the death` IS `living together`
        `years lived together continuously` IS `years together`
        `parent of the deceased's child under 18` IS `parent of a child under 18`
        `age at the death` IS `age`
        `full-time student at the death` IS `student`
        `disability facts` IS `Facts about a disability` WITH
            `attributable to an impairment of the kinds listed` IS `impairment`
            `permanent or likely to be permanent` IS `permanent`
            `substantially reduces capacity in a listed activity` IS `reduced capacity`
            `affects social or economic participation` IS `participation affected`
        `could have taken Family Law Act proceedings` IS `could have taken FLA proceedings`
        `those proceedings were not taken or not finalised` IS `not taken or finalised`
        `now prevented by the death` IS `prevented by death`
        `the deceased's child (my partner) has died` IS `child has died`
        `the day the deceased's child died` IS `day child died`
        `a member of the deceased's household at the death` IS `household member`
        `a past member likely to have become one again` IS `past household member likely to return`

§§ `When a person with a claim, or a person who is liable, has died (s 29, s 30)`
-- Situation: arises only where someone with a claim, or someone a claim is against, has died.

@export Does a claim (cause of action) survive the death of the person who had it or was liable on it, so that it can be brought for or against their estate (Administration and Probate Act 1958 s 29(1), (5))?
GIVEN `kind of claim` IS A `Kind of cause of action` @desc What the claim is: a claim for injury, loss or damage of any other kind; a dependants' claim under Part III of the Wrongs Act 1958; defamation; seduction; or inducing one spouse to leave or remain apart from the other
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does the claim survive the death` MEANS
    `s 29(1) -- the cause of action survives the death` `kind of claim`

@export Is the illness a dust-related condition (Administration and Probate Act 1958 s 3(1) and First Schedule), such as asbestosis, mesothelioma or silicosis?
GIVEN `the condition` IS A `Condition` @desc The illness: one of the fourteen First Schedule diseases (Aluminosis, Asbestosis, Asbestos induced carcinoma, Asbestos related pleural diseases, Bagossosis, Berylliosis, Byssinosis, Coal dust pneumoconiosis, Farmers' lung, Hard metal pneumoconiosis, Mesothelioma, Silicosis, Silico-tuberculosis, Talcosis), another pathological condition of the lungs, pleura, peritoneum or sinus, or a condition of another kind
      `attributable to dust` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For another condition of the lungs, pleura, peritoneum or sinus: whether it is attributable to dust (answer no otherwise)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Is the illness a dust-related condition` MEANS
    `s 3 -- a dust-related condition` `the condition` `attributable to dust`

@export My relative had a claim and has died: can their estate recover this kind of damages, such as pain and suffering for a dust disease (Administration and Probate Act 1958 s 29(2), (2A), s 99AC)?
GIVEN `kind of claim` IS A `Kind of cause of action` @desc What the claim is (defamation, seduction and inducing a spouse to leave do not survive)
      `head of damages` IS A `Head of damages` @desc The kind of damages asked about: exemplary damages, funeral expenses, a loss or gain to the estate because of the death, pain or suffering, bodily or mental harm, the curtailment of expectation of life, future earnings had they survived, loss of earning capacity after the death, or any other loss suffered before the death
      `death caused by the wrong` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your relative's death was caused by the act or omission the claim is about
      `the condition` IS A `Condition` @desc The illness your relative died from (a First Schedule disease, another condition of the lungs, pleura, peritoneum or sinus, or another kind)
      `attributable to dust` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For another condition of the lungs, pleura, peritoneum or sinus: whether it is attributable to dust
      `condition caused by the wrong` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that illness was caused by the act or omission the claim is about
      `proceedings pending at death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your relative had started court proceedings on the claim before they died, and those proceedings were still pending at their death
      `day proceedings started` IS A DATE @desc The day your relative started those proceedings (any day if they did not)
      `day of death` IS A DATE @desc The day your relative died
GIVETH A `What s 29 says of a head of damages`
`Can the estate recover this kind of damages` MEANS
    `s 29(2) -- what the estate may recover for` `kind of claim` `head of damages` `death caused by the wrong`
        (`s 29(2A) -- the dust disease exception applies` `death caused by the wrong` `the condition` `attributable to dust` `condition caused by the wrong` `proceedings pending at death` `day proceedings started` `day of death`)

@export The person who injured me (or who is liable to me in tort) has died: can I still bring or continue proceedings against their estate on the day I name (Administration and Probate Act 1958 s 29(3))?
GIVEN `claim in tort` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is in tort (for example negligence, nuisance, trespass); answer no for a claim in contract only
      `started before their death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you had started proceedings against them before they died, still pending and not out of time at their death
      `my last day had they lived` IS A DATE @desc The last day you could have started proceedings against them had they lived (the limitation period's last day)
      `out of time at their death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your time to sue them had already run out on the day they died
      `representation taken out` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether probate or letters of administration of their estate have been granted
      `day representation taken out` IS A DATE @desc The day probate or letters of administration were granted (any day if not yet)
      `personal injury claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the claim is for negligence, nuisance or breach of duty and includes damages for personal injuries
      `court allowed longer period` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a court, on your application, has decided a longer period is just and reasonable
      `last day of longer period` IS A DATE @desc The last day of that longer period (any day if none)
      `day I start` IS A DATE @desc The day you start (or started) proceedings against their personal representative
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I sue the estate of the person liable to me` MEANS
    `s 29(3) -- I may maintain proceedings against the estate` `claim in tort` `started before their death` `my last day had they lived` `out of time at their death` `representation taken out` `day representation taken out` `personal injury claim` `court allowed longer period` `last day of longer period` `day I start`

@export The person liable to me in tort has died: by when must I start proceedings against their estate, without a court's longer period (Administration and Probate Act 1958 s 29(3)(b)(i), (c))?
GIVEN `my last day had they lived` IS A DATE @desc The last day you could have started proceedings against them had they lived
      `out of time at their death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your time to sue them had already run out on the day they died
      `representation taken out` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether probate or letters of administration of their estate have been granted
      `day representation taken out` IS A DATE @desc The day probate or letters of administration were granted (any day if not yet)
GIVETH A `Last day to sue the estate`
`By when must I sue the estate` MEANS
    `s 29(3) -- my last day to sue the estate` `my last day had they lived` `out of time at their death` `representation taken out` `day representation taken out`

@export The executor or administrator has served me a notice to take proceedings on my claim against the estate: by when must I take them (Administration and Probate Act 1958 s 30(1))?
GIVEN `day I received the notice` IS A DATE @desc The day you received the personal representative's notice
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I take proceedings after the executor's notice` MEANS
    `s 30 -- the last day to take proceedings after the notice` `day I received the notice`

@export Have I taken proceedings in time after the executor's or administrator's notice, or is there still time (Administration and Probate Act 1958 s 30)? If not, the Court may bar the claim on their application.
GIVEN `day I received the notice` IS A DATE @desc The day you received the personal representative's notice
      `proceedings taken` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have taken proceedings to enforce or establish your claim
      `day proceedings taken` IS A DATE @desc The day you took them (any day if not yet)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `What became of the step`
`Have I taken proceedings in time after the executor's notice` MEANS
    `s 30 -- what became of my proceedings after the notice` `day I received the notice` `proceedings taken` `day proceedings taken` `today`

§§ `When the person who died left no will, or a will that does not deal with everything (Part IA)`
-- Situation: arises only where the person who died left no will, or a will that does not dispose of the whole estate.

@export Do the intestacy rules decide who gets the estate (Administration and Probate Act 1958 s 70A, s 106)?
GIVEN `day of death` IS A DATE @desc The day the person died (deaths before 1 November 2017 fall under the earlier rules)
      `what they left` IS A `What the person left` @desc No will; a will that does not effectively dispose of the whole estate; or a will that effectively disposes of the whole estate
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Do the intestacy rules apply` MEANS
    `s 70A -- Part IA applies` `day of death` `what they left`

@export Did I (or the relative I ask about) survive the person who died long enough to share in the estate under the intestacy rules (Administration and Probate Act 1958 s 70C)?
GIVEN `day of their death` IS A DATE @desc The day the person who died intestate died
      `I have died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person asking about (you or your relative) has since died
      `day I died` IS A DATE @desc The day that person died (any day if they have not)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
      `otherwise to the Crown` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, without this person, the estate would pass to the Crown (there is no other relative entitled)
GIVETH A `Survival under s 70C`
`Did I survive long enough to share` MEANS
    `s 70C -- survival` `day of their death` `I have died` `day I died` `today` `otherwise to the Crown`

@export Who takes the estate under the intestacy rules: the partner, the children, parents, siblings, grandparents, aunts and uncles, or the Crown (Administration and Probate Act 1958 ss 70J-70ZL)?
GIVEN `partners` IS A NUMBER @desc How many partners (spouse, domestic partner or registered caring partner at the death) survived the person by 30 days
      `child lines` IS A NUMBER @desc How many children survived by 30 days, plus children who died before leaving a child or grandchild who survived
      `issue not of a partner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether any of those children or grandchildren is not also a child or grandchild of the partner (with several partners: of any of them)
      `parents` IS A NUMBER @desc How many parents survived by 30 days
      `sibling lines` IS A NUMBER @desc How many brothers and sisters survived by 30 days, plus brothers and sisters who died before leaving a child who survived
      `remote sibling issue` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a grandchild or remoter descendant of a brother or sister survived (only matters where no brother, sister or their child did)
      `grandparents` IS A NUMBER @desc How many grandparents survived by 30 days
      `aunt and uncle lines` IS A NUMBER @desc How many aunts and uncles survived by 30 days, plus aunts and uncles who died before leaving a child (cousin) who survived
      `estate worth` IS A NUMBER @desc What the residuary estate is worth after funeral and administration expenses, debts and pecuniary legacies, in dollars
      `statutory legacy` IS A NUMBER @desc The partner's statutory legacy for the year, in dollars ($451,909 under s 70M(1); later years by the Minister's Gazette notice)
GIVETH A `Who takes the residuary estate`
`Who takes the estate` MEANS
    `Part IA -- who takes the residuary estate` (`the family of` `partners` `child lines` `issue not of a partner` `parents` `sibling lines` `remote sibling issue` `grandparents` `aunt and uncle lines`) `estate worth` `statutory legacy`

@export What do I take from the estate as the partner (spouse, domestic partner or registered caring partner) under the intestacy rules (Administration and Probate Act 1958 ss 70J-70L, 70Z-70ZE)?
GIVEN `partners` IS A NUMBER @desc How many partners, you included, survived the person by 30 days
      `child lines` IS A NUMBER @desc How many children survived by 30 days, plus children who died before leaving a child or grandchild who survived
      `issue not of a partner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether any of those children or grandchildren is not also your child or grandchild (with several partners: not of any partner)
      `estate worth` IS A NUMBER @desc What the residuary estate is worth, chattels included, in dollars
      `chattels worth` IS A NUMBER @desc What the personal chattels (household and personal effects, cars, not business assets or money) are worth, in dollars
      `statutory legacy` IS A NUMBER @desc The partner's statutory legacy for the year, in dollars
      `agreement or order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc With several partners: whether the partners have a written distribution agreement, or the Court has made a distribution order
GIVETH A MAYBE `A partner's entitlement`
`What do I take as the partner` MEANS
    `Part IA -- what I take as a partner` (`the family of` `partners` `child lines` `issue not of a partner` 0 0 FALSE 0 0) `estate worth` `chattels worth` `statutory legacy` `agreement or order`

@export What do I take from the estate as a child, grandchild, parent, sibling, nephew or niece, grandparent, aunt or uncle, or cousin under the intestacy rules (Administration and Probate Act 1958 ss 70L(2), (3), 70ZB(2), (3), 70ZG-70ZK)?
GIVEN `my place` IS A `My place in the family` @desc Your place: a child, grandchild or remoter descendant; a parent; a sibling or the child of a sibling who died; a grandparent; an aunt or uncle or the child (cousin) of one who died
      `lines further down` IS A LIST OF NUMBER @desc Empty if you take in your own right (a child, parent, sibling, grandparent, aunt or uncle). Otherwise, for each generation between, the number of lines in that family: a grandchild whose parent died gives [the number of your parent's children who survived or left issue]; a nephew or niece gives [the number of children of your parent who survived]
      `partners` IS A NUMBER @desc How many partners survived the person by 30 days
      `child lines` IS A NUMBER @desc How many children survived by 30 days, plus children who died before leaving a child or grandchild who survived
      `issue not of a partner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether any child or grandchild is not also a child or grandchild of the partner (or of any partner)
      `parents` IS A NUMBER @desc How many parents survived by 30 days
      `sibling lines` IS A NUMBER @desc How many brothers and sisters survived by 30 days, plus those who died before leaving a child who survived
      `remote sibling issue` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a grandchild or remoter descendant of a brother or sister survived
      `grandparents` IS A NUMBER @desc How many grandparents survived by 30 days
      `aunt and uncle lines` IS A NUMBER @desc How many aunts and uncles survived by 30 days, plus those who died before leaving a child who survived
      `estate worth` IS A NUMBER @desc What the residuary estate is worth, chattels included, in dollars
      `chattels worth` IS A NUMBER @desc What the personal chattels are worth, in dollars
      `statutory legacy` IS A NUMBER @desc The partner's statutory legacy for the year, in dollars
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`What do I take as a relative` MEANS
    `Part IA -- what I take as a relative` (`the family of` `partners` `child lines` `issue not of a partner` `parents` `sibling lines` `remote sibling issue` `grandparents` `aunt and uncle lines`) `estate worth` `chattels worth` `statutory legacy` `my place` `lines further down`

@export As the partner, how much interest is added to my statutory legacy, from the death to the day it is paid (Administration and Probate Act 1958 ss 3(1), 70L(1)(b)(iii), 70ZB(1)(c)(ii))?
GIVEN `statutory legacy` IS A NUMBER @desc The statutory legacy (or, with several partners, your part of it), in dollars
      `cash rate` IS A NUMBER @desc The Reserve Bank of Australia cash rate last published before 1 January of the year of the death, in per cent (e.g. 4.35)
      `day of death` IS A DATE @desc The day the person died
      `day of payment` IS A DATE @desc The day the legacy is (or will be) paid
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much interest is added to my statutory legacy` MEANS
    `s 70L(1)(b)(iii) -- interest on the statutory legacy` `statutory legacy` `cash rate` `day of death` `day of payment`

@export What is the partner's statutory legacy for a financial year, from last year's figures and the Melbourne consumer price index (Administration and Probate Act 1958 s 70M)?
GIVEN `amount last calculated` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount last calculated under the s 70M formula, in dollars (0 if none yet)
      `index B` IS A NUMBER @desc The Melbourne all groups CPI for the latest reference period in the preceding calendar year, as published at the 15 June before the variation
      `index C` IS A NUMBER @desc The Melbourne all groups CPI for the same reference period a year earlier
      `amount in effect last year` IS A NUMBER @desc The statutory legacy in effect last financial year, in dollars ($451,909 for the first)
GIVETH A `The statutory legacy for a year`
`What is the partner's statutory legacy for the year` MEANS
    `s 70M(2)-(4) -- the partner's statutory legacy for the year` `amount last calculated` `index B` `index C` `amount in effect last year`

@export As the only partner of a person who died intestate, can I elect to buy property from the estate, such as the family home (Administration and Probate Act 1958 ss 70O, 70P)?
GIVEN `partners` IS A NUMBER @desc How many partners, you included, survived the person by 30 days
      `child lines` IS A NUMBER @desc How many children survived by 30 days, plus children who died before leaving a child or grandchild who survived
      `issue not of a partner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether any of those children or grandchildren is not also your child or grandchild
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I make a partner's property election` MEANS
    `s 70O -- I may make a partner's property election` (`the family of` `partners` `child lines` `issue not of a partner` 0 0 FALSE 0 0)

@export As the partner, by when must the administrator give me written notice of my right to elect to acquire estate property (Administration and Probate Act 1958 s 70Q)?
GIVEN `I am the administrator` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are yourself a personal representative (administrator) of the estate
      `day of the grant` IS A DATE @desc The day letters of administration were granted
GIVETH A `The right to elect notice`
`By when must I be given the right to elect notice` MEANS
    `s 70Q -- when I must be given the right to elect notice` `I am the administrator` `day of the grant`

@export As the partner, was I given the right to elect notice in time, or is there still time (Administration and Probate Act 1958 s 70Q)?
GIVEN `day of the grant` IS A DATE @desc The day letters of administration were granted
      `notice given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the administrator has given you the written notice
      `day notice given` IS A DATE @desc The day it was given (any day if not yet)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `What became of the step`
`Was I given the right to elect notice in time` MEANS
    `s 70Q -- what became of the right to elect notice` `day of the grant` `notice given` `day notice given` `today`

@export As the partner, by when must I make my election to acquire property from the estate (Administration and Probate Act 1958 s 70R)?
GIVEN `I am the administrator` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are yourself a personal representative (administrator) of the estate
      `day of the grant` IS A DATE @desc The day letters of administration were granted
      `notice given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have been given the right to elect notice
      `date of the notice` IS A DATE @desc The date of the right to elect notice (any day if none)
      `Court extended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has extended the time for your election
      `extended to` IS A DATE @desc The date to which the Court extended it (any day if not)
GIVETH A `Last day for the election`
`By when must I make my property election` MEANS
    `s 70R -- my last day to elect` `I am the administrator` `day of the grant` `notice given` `date of the notice` `Court extended` `extended to`

@export As the partner, is my election to acquire property from the estate in time, or is there still time (Administration and Probate Act 1958 s 70R)?
GIVEN `I am the administrator` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are yourself a personal representative (administrator) of the estate
      `day of the grant` IS A DATE @desc The day letters of administration were granted
      `notice given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have been given the right to elect notice
      `date of the notice` IS A DATE @desc The date of the right to elect notice (any day if none)
      `Court extended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has extended the time for your election
      `extended to` IS A DATE @desc The date to which the Court extended it (any day if not)
      `election made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have made the election, in writing
      `day of election` IS A DATE @desc The day you made it (any day if not yet)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A MAYBE `What became of the step`
`Is my property election in time` MEANS
    `s 70R -- what became of my election` `I am the administrator` `day of the grant` `notice given` `date of the notice` `Court extended` `extended to` `election made` `day of election` `today`

@export As the partner who elected to acquire estate property, what must I pay into the estate, and by when (Administration and Probate Act 1958 s 70W)?
GIVEN `price` IS A NUMBER @desc The price of the property (its value at the death, or the price agreed with the other beneficiaries), in dollars
      `my share` IS A NUMBER @desc What your share of the residuary estate is worth, in dollars
      `day of election` IS A DATE @desc The day you made the election
      `distribution day set` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a day has been set for the distribution of the residuary estate
      `distribution day` IS A DATE @desc That day (any day if none)
GIVETH A `What I must pay into the estate`
`What must I pay into the estate for the property` MEANS
    `s 70W(2) -- what I must pay into the estate, and by when` `price` `my share` `day of election` `distribution day set` `distribution day`

§§ `When the will or the intestacy rules leave me without proper provision (Part IV)`
-- Situation: arises only where the person asking was close to the person who died, or depended on them.

@export Am I an eligible person who may apply for a family provision order from the estate of a person who died (Administration and Probate Act 1958 ss 3, 90)?
GIVEN `relationship` IS A `My relationship to the deceased` @desc Your relationship to the person who died: spouse at the death; living with them as a couple or a registered domestic partner; child (including adopted); stepchild; a person who believed they were your parent and treated you as their natural child; former spouse or former domestic partner; registered caring partner; grandchild; spouse or domestic partner of their child; or none of these
      `day of death` IS A DATE @desc The day the person died
      `registered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were in a registered domestic relationship with them (Relationships Act 2008)
      `living together` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were living with them as a couple on a genuine domestic basis at their death
      `years together` IS A NUMBER @desc How many years you had lived with them in that way continuously, up to their death
      `parent of a child under 18` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the parent of a child of theirs who was under 18 at their death
      `age` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age in whole years at their death
      `student` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were a full-time student at their death
      `impairment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have a disability attributable to intellectual, cognitive, neurological, sensory or physical impairments, or to a psychiatric condition
      `permanent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether those impairments are, or are likely to be, permanent
      `reduced capacity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they substantially reduce your capacity in communication, social interaction, learning, mobility, self-care or self-management
      `participation affected` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they affect your capacity for social or economic participation
      `could have taken FLA proceedings` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Former partners: whether at the death you could have taken property or maintenance proceedings under the Family Law Act 1975
      `not taken or finalised` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Former partners: whether you had not taken them, or had started but not finalised them
      `prevented by death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Former partners: whether the death now prevents you taking or finalising them
      `child has died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Partners of the deceased's child: whether that child (your spouse or partner) has died
      `day child died` IS A DATE @desc The day that child died (any day if not)
      `household member` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were a member of the same household as the person at their death
      `past household member likely to return` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you had been a member of their household in the past and would likely have become one again soon had they not died
GIVETH AN `Eligible person`
`Am I an eligible person` MEANS
    `s 90 -- the kind of eligible person I am` (`the facts about me` `relationship` `day of death` `registered` `living together` `years together` `parent of a child under 18` `age` `student` `impairment` `permanent` `reduced capacity` `participation affected` `could have taken FLA proceedings` `not taken or finalised` `prevented by death` `child has died` `day child died` `household member` `past household member likely to return`)

@export Can the Court make a family provision order for me out of the estate of the person who died (Administration and Probate Act 1958 ss 90A, 91(1)-(3), 101)? The Court decides; this says whether the conditions are met.
GIVEN `relationship` IS A `My relationship to the deceased` @desc Your relationship to the person who died (as for the eligibility question)
      `day of death` IS A DATE @desc The day the person died (deaths before 1 January 2015 fall under the earlier Part IV)
      `registered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were in a registered domestic relationship with them
      `living together` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were living with them as a couple at their death
      `years together` IS A NUMBER @desc How many years you had lived with them in that way continuously, up to their death
      `parent of a child under 18` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the parent of a child of theirs who was under 18 at their death
      `age` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age in whole years at their death
      `student` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were a full-time student at their death
      `impairment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have a disability attributable to listed impairments or a psychiatric condition
      `permanent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether those impairments are, or are likely to be, permanent
      `reduced capacity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they substantially reduce your capacity in a listed activity
      `participation affected` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they affect your capacity for social or economic participation
      `could have taken FLA proceedings` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Former partners: whether you could have taken Family Law Act proceedings at the death
      `not taken or finalised` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Former partners: whether you had not taken them, or not finalised them
      `prevented by death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Former partners: whether the death now prevents them
      `child has died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Partners of the deceased's child: whether that child has died
      `day child died` IS A DATE @desc The day that child died (any day if not)
      `household member` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were a member of their household at their death
      `past household member likely to return` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you had been in their household and would likely have rejoined it
      `dependent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were wholly or partly dependent on them for your proper maintenance and support (leave out government means-tested benefits)
      `moral duty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied that, at the death, they had a moral duty to provide for your proper maintenance and support
      `inadequate provision` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied that the will, the intestacy rules or both fail to make adequate provision for your proper maintenance and support
      `in time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your application is made within 6 months of the grant, or the Court has extended the time
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the Court make a family provision order for me` MEANS
    `s 91 -- the Court may make a family provision order for me` (`the facts about me` `relationship` `day of death` `registered` `living together` `years together` `parent of a child under 18` `age` `student` `impairment` `permanent` `reduced capacity` `participation affected` `could have taken FLA proceedings` `not taken or finalised` `prevented by death` `child has died` `day child died` `household member` `past household member likely to return`) `dependent` `moral duty` `inadequate provision` `in time`

@export What must the Court weigh in fixing the amount of a family provision order for me, and what limits that amount (Administration and Probate Act 1958 s 91(4), (5))?
GIVEN `relationship` IS A `My relationship to the deceased` @desc Your relationship to the person who died (as for the eligibility question)
      `day of death` IS A DATE @desc The day the person died
      `registered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were in a registered domestic relationship with them
      `living together` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were living with them as a couple at their death
      `years together` IS A NUMBER @desc How many years you had lived with them in that way continuously, up to their death
      `parent of a child under 18` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the parent of a child of theirs who was under 18 at their death
      `age` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age in whole years at their death
      `student` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were a full-time student at their death
      `impairment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have a disability attributable to listed impairments or a psychiatric condition
      `permanent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether those impairments are, or are likely to be, permanent
      `reduced capacity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they substantially reduce your capacity in a listed activity
      `participation affected` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they affect your capacity for social or economic participation
      `could have taken FLA proceedings` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Former partners: whether you could have taken Family Law Act proceedings at the death
      `not taken or finalised` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Former partners: whether you had not taken them, or not finalised them
      `prevented by death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Former partners: whether the death now prevents them
      `child has died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Partners of the deceased's child: whether that child has died
      `day child died` IS A DATE @desc The day that child died (any day if not)
      `household member` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were a member of their household at their death
      `past household member likely to return` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you had been in their household and would likely have rejoined it
GIVETH A LIST OF `What the Court must weigh or keep to`
`What must the Court weigh in fixing my provision` MEANS
    `s 91(4), (5) -- what the Court must weigh, and the limits on my provision` (`the facts about me` `relationship` `day of death` `registered` `living together` `years together` `parent of a child under 18` `age` `student` `impairment` `permanent` `reduced capacity` `participation affected` `could have taken FLA proceedings` `not taken or finalised` `prevented by death` `child has died` `day child died` `household member` `past household member likely to return`)

@export By when must I apply for a family provision order (Administration and Probate Act 1958 s 99(1))?
GIVEN `day of the grant` IS A DATE @desc The day probate of the will or letters of administration were granted
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I apply for family provision` MEANS
    `s 99(1) -- my last day to apply` `day of the grant`

@export Is my family provision application in time, still open, or late, and can the time still be extended (Administration and Probate Act 1958 s 99)?
GIVEN `day of the grant` IS A DATE @desc The day probate or letters of administration were granted
      `applied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have applied to the Court
      `day applied` IS A DATE @desc The day you applied (any day if not yet)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
      `Court extended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has extended the period for your application
      `finally distributed first` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the estate was finally distributed before you applied for an extension (or has been, if you have not)
GIVETH A `My family provision application`
`Where does my family provision application stand` MEANS
    `s 99 -- where my application stands` `day of the grant` `applied` `day applied` `today` `Court extended` `finally distributed first`

@export I gave the executor or administrator written notice that I intend to apply for family provision: when does that notice lapse if I have not applied (Administration and Probate Act 1958 s 99A(4))?
GIVEN `day received` IS A DATE @desc The day the personal representative received your notice of intention
GIVETH A DATE
`When does my notice of intention lapse` MEANS
    `s 99A(4) -- the last day before my notice of intention lapses` `day received`

@export The executor or administrator has distributed part of the estate: can I proceed against them, or disturb the distribution, by my family provision claim (Administration and Probate Act 1958 s 99A)?
GIVEN `properly made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the distribution was properly made by the personal representative
      `for maintenance of partner or dependent child` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was made to provide for the maintenance, support or education of the partner, or of a child who was wholly or partly dependent on the deceased
      `I consented in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you (of full legal capacity) had told the personal representative in writing that you consent to the distribution or do not intend to apply
      `day of the grant` IS A DATE @desc The day probate or letters of administration were granted
      `day of distribution` IS A DATE @desc The day the distribution was made
      `notice of an application` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the personal representative had notice of a family provision application before the distribution
      `notice of intention given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you gave the personal representative a signed written notice of your intention to apply
      `day notice received` IS A DATE @desc The day the personal representative received that notice (any day if none)
      `applied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have applied to the Court
      `day applied` IS A DATE @desc The day you applied (any day if not)
      `written notice of application received` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the personal representative received written notice that you applied
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I proceed against the executor over a distribution` MEANS
    `s 99A -- I may proceed against the personal representative over the distribution`
        (`Facts about a distribution` WITH
            `the distribution was properly made` IS `properly made`
            `it was for the maintenance, support or education of the partner or a dependent child` IS `for maintenance of partner or dependent child`
            `I had notified in writing my consent or that I would not apply` IS `I consented in writing`
            `the day of the grant` IS `day of the grant`
            `the day of the distribution` IS `day of distribution`
            `the personal representative had notice of an application` IS `notice of an application`
            `I gave a notice of intention to apply` IS `notice of intention given`
            `the day the personal representative received my notice` IS `day notice received`
            `I have applied` IS `applied`
            `the day I applied` IS `day applied`
            `the personal representative received written notice of my application` IS `written notice of application received`)
